2. Check the timers at the top of the GUI
<<<
EXPECTED_BEHAVIOR: >>>
1. Verify the emission started
2. Verify the timers started to increase
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWT-6
  ROLE: Verifies

[TEST_CASE]
UID: TC-H1FWT-20
TITLE: Timer Functionality 4 - Test Case
STATEMENT: >>>
Req:

The timer firmware shall set a latching fault status interlock signal if the timeout expires
Methode:

The tester will load a variant of Main Control FW where the main timer won’t stop counting. The backup timers will stop the emission and latch the fault even after the timeout expires
<<<
PREREQUISITES: >>>
1 - Main Control FW variant <#TIMER_TEST_MODE> is loaded
<<<
STEPS: >>>
1. Prepare a treatment plan on the internal application:
• Energy: 50kV
• Dwell time: 20 sec
• Collimator: any
• Pixels: any

2. Load the treatment plan to the external application
3. Execute the treatment plan
4. Wait for 10 seconds after the completion of the execution
<<<
EXPECTED_BEHAVIOR: >>>
1. Emission stops after 20 seconds +/- 1 sec with a timer fault
2. The timer fault still exists
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWT-9
  ROLE: Verifies

[TEST_CASE]
UID: TC-H1FWT-22
TITLE: Timer Characteristics 2 - Test Case
STATEMENT: >>>
When user has started to run a normal 20 second treatment plan, make sure the timers’ values increased and have a reported resolution of 0.01 seconds.
<<<
PREREQUISITES: >>>
1. A treatment plan is loaded and ready for run about 20 seconds
2. The system is ready to run without any issues.
<<<
STEPS: >>>
1. Start a run for the loaded treatment plan for 20 seconds.
2. Check the timers on GUI
3. Check timeout counter
<<<
EXPECTED_BEHAVIOR: >>>
1. Verify the emission started without any issues
2. Verify the timers’ values increased and have a reported resolution of 0.01 seconds
3. Verify it has a minimum resolution of 60 milliseconds or less.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWT-11
  ROLE: Verifies

