[TEST_CASE]
UID: TC-H1FWT-24
TITLE: Timer Functionality 6 - Test Case
STATEMENT: >>>
The timers won’t increase when the emission stops.
<<<
PREREQUISITES: >>>
1. A treatment plan is loaded and ready for run
2. The system is ready to run without any issues.
<<<
STEPS: >>>
1. Run a normal treatment of 20 seconds
2. After 5 seconds, stop through GUI by clicking the STOP button.
3. Check the timers
<<<
EXPECTED_BEHAVIOR: >>>
1. Verify the emission started
2. Verify the emission stopped
3. Verify the timers won’t increase and stops counting
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWT-13
  ROLE: Verifies

[TEST_CASE]
UID: TC-H1FWT-25
TITLE: Timer Functionality 7 - Test Case
STATEMENT: >>>
When a user starts to run a treatment plan, verify backup timers can terminate the emission accordingly.
<<<
PREREQUISITES: >>>
1. A treatment plan is loaded and ready for run
2. The system is ready to run without any issues.
<<<
STEPS: >>>
1. Run a normal treatment of 20 seconds
2. After 20 seconds, check the emission.
<<<
EXPECTED_BEHAVIOR: >>>
1. Verify the emission started
2. Verify the emission stopped at 20 seconds +/- 10% because of the backup timers.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWT-14
  ROLE: Verifies

[TEST_CASE]
UID: TC-H1FWT-26
TITLE: Timer Functionality 3 - Test Case
STATEMENT: >>>
Req:

The firmware shall clear any latched faults when the timer value is reset by the main control microcontroller

Methode:

The tester will load a variant of Main Control FW where the main timer won’t stop counting. The back timers will stop the emission and latch the fault even after the timeout expires. Only a reset from the main control board will reset the fault.
<<<
PREREQUISITES: >>>
1. Main Control FW variant <#TIMER_TEST_MODE> is loaded
<<<
STEPS: >>>
1. Prepare a treatment plan on the internal application:
• Energy: 50kV
• Dwell time: 20 sec
• Collimator: any
• Pixels: any
2. Load the treatment plan to the external application
3. Execute the treatment plan
4. Check for a fault related to timers
5. Reset the fault
<<<
EXPECTED_BEHAVIOR: >>>
1. Emission stops after 20
seconds +/- 1 sec
2. The fault still exists
3. The fault is cleared
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWT-4
  ROLE: Verifies

[TEST_CASE]
UID: TC-H1FWT-27
TITLE: Timer Characteristics 3 - Test Case
STATEMENT: >>>
When a user starts to run the loaded treatment plan, it shouldn’t stop the treatment because of the special version of the firmware application.
<<<
PREREQUISITES: >>>
1. Specific version of firmware application is loaded which its main timer doesn’t stop the treatment.
2. The system is ready to use and run a loaded treatment plan
3. A treatment plan is loaded into the system.
<<<
STEPS: >>>
1. Run the loaded treatment plan using a point of duration 20 seconds.
2. Wait for at least 1 minute without clearing errors or resetting timers.
<<<
EXPECTED_BEHAVIOR: >>>
1. Verify the emission started. Also, verify that the backup timers terminate the emission at 20 seconds +/- 10%
2. Verify that the timers’ values on the screen do not continue to count
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWT-1
  ROLE: Verifies

[TEST_CASE]
UID: TC-H1FWT-59
TITLE: Timer Address Test
STATEMENT: >>>
The timers have a unique I2C address each, they need to be verified to be true and distinct.
<<<
PREREQUISITES: >>>
1. Specific version of firmware application is loaded which its main timer doesn’t stop the treatment.
2. The system is ready to use and run a loaded treatment plan
3. A treatment plan is loaded into the system.
<<<
STEPS: >>>
1. Send valid data packet to each Timer with correct I2C address in C file and validate
2. Send the opposite addresses to each Timer and ensure communication fails
3. Send incorrect addresses to the Timer and ensure communication fails
<<<
EXPECTED_BEHAVIOR: >>>
1. Timer data integrity is maintained in the response
2. ERROR - No timer data is output
3. ERROR - No timer data is output
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SW-H1FWT-58
  ROLE: Verifies

[[/SECTION]]
